![Photo by ThisisEngineering RAEng on Unsplash](cover.jpg)

Bootcamps are schools where they teach you a set of tools with the
goal of improving your skills and making your entry into the IT
industry faster. A common question is: Is a Bootcamp worth it for
me? Given the various options, this short article could help you
determine whether a Bootcamp is something you want to experience.

## What is your profile?

The first thing to determine is what profile you have.

### 1. Beginner

No experience or prior knowledge. You come from another industry or
have never done any computing project.

A Bootcamp could help you define your path and advise you on the
technologies you can learn. It also introduces you to a small set of
projects you can build.

However, first consider studying a specific course (free or paid)
in some technology so you can probe the terrain before a
significant investment of time and money.

I would personally recommend a low-cost state course like
[Talento Digital](https://talentodigitalparachile.cl/).

Remember that Bootcamps are not mandatory to enter the industry or
to learn the technologies.

### 2. Intermediate

You have done some projects (without following tutorials) but have
very little professional experience in the industry.

Only join a Bootcamp if you truly feel it will give you relevant
support and value.

If you already have some knowledge, the recommendation is to build
freelance projects or help NGOs to sharpen and grease the tools.

Having small projects for clients will give you greater incentives
to learn the tools and become an expert.

### 3. Expert

You have studied an industry degree or have relevant work
experience in it (more than two years).

The truth is a Bootcamp won't help you much. You're better off with
specific technology courses or practice. Building projects on your
own, improving your soft skills, and expanding your network will be
more fruitful.

## Do you have the Money?

A Bootcamp is a large investment of money and time. Never get into
debt (with a bank) to study in one. Going to a Bootcamp gives you
no guarantee of finding a job or starting on a millionaire salary.
It is not a silver bullet that gives you the keys to enter the
industry — they are simply training schools.

I would honestly recommend at least two years of study. A Bootcamp
runs between 6 to 12 months.

If you have the resources to afford a technical degree, I would
recommend going to a university or certified institute before a
Bootcamp.

## What is its prestige?

Many companies today have filters on the quality of graduates from
certain institutions. Make sure wherever you study has a good
reputation in the industry and that its graduates have good
recommendations about the institution. The prestige of where you
studied can work for or against you, especially in the first years
when you don't have much work experience to back your abilities.

## Do they have support networks?

A Bootcamp should be able to help you land your first job in the
industry. Whether by giving you companies and internships or at
minimum the ability to get interviews. If they don't give you that,
they are no different from an in-person or online course at some
[OTEC](https://www.chileatiende.gob.cl/fichas/39693-organismos-tecnicos-de-capacitacion-otec).

Besides technical knowledge, a Bootcamp should also advise you on
building your resume and training you on how to do job interviews
and networking.

## Learn the Fundamentals

Many Bootcamps focus on popular technologies like JavaScript and
Python. But remember that technologies are ephemeral — the industry
changes as fast as the wind's direction, so it is very important
that you learn the fundamentals that will let you adapt to any
technology or industry.

I personally recommend the [Elixir](https://elixir-lang.org)
technology for its functional paradigm; it will let you step outside
the norm and build projects across a wide range of contexts.

While this technology is not as well known as JavaScript and Python
(yet), it can serve you well to learn Algorithms, Web Systems,
Mobile Apps, Robotics, Machine Learning and many other use cases.

Follow the [Elixir Track on Exercism](https://exercism.org/tracks/elixir/)
to practice exercises that will help you improve your skills.

You could start with technologies like Pascal, C and Assembler to
learn the fundamentals — highly recommended. But honestly I believe
_Elixir_ is a relevant and modern technology with a high future and
growth potential. I personally see it as a safe bet.

Nothing prevents you from going for other technologies like
JavaScript once you know the fundamentals — having several tools
under your arm will give you greater chances to stand out and
personal confidence that we can succeed in the wonderful IT
industry.
